{
  "description": "The first of the two reasons spec/execution-model.md:660-664 gives for the rule, in its `stop` form: \"a `return` in a handler firing while the registering call was still on the stack would be consumed as that call's own result\". The `\"start\"` handler fires synchronously during registration, so `setup` IS still on the stack — and the `stop` must still be reported as outside any procedure (`ol-stop-outside-proc`, spec/error-model.md:119) rather than quietly ending `setup`. Its sibling `a-deferred-stop-outlives-the-procedure-that-registered-it` covers the second reason, a firing after the call returned. Added at the QA reviewer's request during review round 3, which measured that removing ONLY the handler boundary's `stop` conversion — leaving its `return` conversion intact — survives all 1035 fixtures: the program then emits a `procedure-exit` and no diagnostic at all, the `stop` swallowed as `setup`'s own. `interaction-events/when/when-return-in-handler-inside-proc` is the `return` cell of the same pair.",
